locus 0 beta 6

meta title = "Agent workflow: never render until check passes"
meta note = "Composition example: concise declarations, reusable functions, separate shapes and labels, centering and containment, relational layout, and automatic routing; supported by the beta 6 measured solver and validated across SVG, TikZ and PNG"

style textbook

// ---- declaration shorthand ----------------------------------
// Proposed kind name(args) expands to kind name = kind(args).
// For example, rect shape() expands to rect shape = rect(), and
// label no(failure, "no") expands to label no = label(failure, "no").
// Constructor overloads, style classes, and properties retain their meaning.
// User-defined functions use the same named-call form to expand a block.

// ---- layout relation set (20 names) --------------
// center(content, container) aligns both measured centers without sizing.
// contain(container, content...) encloses content with style padding.
// Matching dimensions use ordinary numeric equality on width and height.
// gap_x(A, B, ...) separates adjacent full bounds from left to right.
// gap_y(A, B, ...) separates adjacent full bounds from top to bottom.
// Argument order supplies direction; reverse it for the opposite direction.
// Gaps are chosen automatically, need not be equal, and are not numeric inputs.
// Neither gap relation imposes alignment along the other axis.
// row(items...) combines gap_x with align_center_y; column(items...) combines
// gap_y with align_center_x. They expand groups into ordered members first.
// align_left/right/top/bottom(items...) align the corresponding bounds only.
// align_center_x/y(items...) align measured centers on the named axis only.
// Alignment permits overlap and imposes no ordering, separation, or sizing.
// inset_left/right/top/bottom(content, container) align the named content edge
// to the container's inner margin using style padding; the other axis is free.
// Each inset accepts { padding: none } to share that container border instead.
// Containment and center alignment remain separate relations. Two flush insets
// place a corner; one flush inset plus center alignment places a side center.
// cover_x/y(container, items...) cover combined bounds on the named axis with
// style padding; the other dimension and axis placement remain free.
// avoid(subject, obstacles...) keeps geometry clear with automatic clearance.
// trim_endpoints(paths, boundary) clips visible interior endpoints to the
// boundary's outline while retaining logical endpoints and middle crossings.
// Except row and column, these layout relations use a group's
// combined measured bounds. Path operations act on individual group members.
// on, center alignments, and numeric equality are existing relations.
// Not every proposed relation is used in this example.

// ---- reusable functions -------------------------------------
// Proposed fn syntax is syntax sugar for a reusable statement block.
// Parameters are substituted at expansion time; text accepts a text value.
// Define a function before invoking it. There is no return type or value.
// A named invocation, labeled_rect init(...), expands the declarations
// as init.shape and init.caption and rewrites its relations to those ids.
// Each invocation creates fresh objects; all expanded declarations and
// relations participate in the figure's shared solve and rendering.
// The invocation name is a namespace, not another geometric object.
// Qualified references expose the expanded objects and their usual anchors.
fn labeled_rect(text) {
    // rect() leaves placement, width, and height for Locus to choose.
    rect shape()
    // label(text) measures text independently; relations set its placement.
    label caption(text)

    // center(content, container): make the content's measured bounding-box
    // center coincide with the container's center on both axes.
    // This sets alignment only; it does not require the content to fit.
    rel center(caption, shape)

    // contain(container, content): keep the entire measured content box
    // inside the container's actual interior, with padding from the style.
    // Containment does not imply centering. With center and unspecified
    // dimensions, Locus chooses compact sizes satisfying both relations;
    // the style supplies proportions when several sizes would work.
    // External dimension equalities may enlarge the expanded shape further.
    rel contain(shape, caption)
}

fn labeled_diamond(text) {
    // diamond() leaves placement, width, and height for Locus to choose.
    diamond shape()
    label caption(text)
    rel center(caption, shape)
    // Containment accounts for the diamond's sloping sides, not just its bounds.
    rel contain(shape, caption)
}

// ---- nodes --------------------------------------------------
// Each call reuses the definition but creates its own shape and label.
// An invocation's style class is copied to expanded declarations that
// do not specify their own class.
labeled_rect init("locus init")
labeled_rect add("locus add < ops.locus")
labeled_rect check("locus check")
labeled_diamond decision("exit 0?")
labeled_rect render("locus render -o fig.svg") [highlight]
labeled_rect repair("fix ops (update / edit)")

// ---- layout intent ------------------------------------------
// column(A, B, ...): align the objects' centers on a vertical line and
// place them from top to bottom in argument order. Keep adjacent shapes
// separate, with clear gaps chosen by Locus from the active style.
// This relation defines placement only; it does not create connectors.
rel column(init.shape, add.shape, check.shape, decision.shape, render.shape)

// These rectangles share width and height through ordinary measurement
// equalities, without placement or numeric distances. Combined with contain,
// their dimensions must accommodate every label, including the longest one.
rel add.shape.width == init.shape.width and add.shape.height == init.shape.height
rel check.shape.width == init.shape.width and check.shape.height == init.shape.height
rel render.shape.width == init.shape.width and render.shape.height == init.shape.height
rel repair.shape.width == init.shape.width and repair.shape.height == init.shape.height

// gap_x(A, B): place B entirely to the right of A, with a clear gap
// chosen by Locus. Compare the shapes' boundaries, not just their centers.
// This establishes horizontal order without imposing vertical alignment.
rel gap_x(decision.shape, repair.shape)

// align_center_y(A, B): place the centers at the same vertical level.
// Together with gap_x, this puts the repair shape on the decision row
// and makes their east-to-west connector horizontal.
rel align_center_y(repair.shape, decision.shape)

// ---- forward flow -------------------------------------------
arrow initialize(init.shape.s, add.shape.n)
arrow validate(add.shape.s, check.shape.n)
arrow decide(check.shape.s, decision.shape.n)

// Proposed label overload: label(arrow, text) attaches text to a connector.
// near: start prefers a position near the arrow's source, along its path.
// side: left means the page's left side of this downward connector;
// Locus chooses the offset and keeps the label clear of strokes and shapes.
arrow success(decision.shape.s, render.shape.n)
label yes(success, "yes") { near: start, side: left }

arrow failure(decision.shape.e, repair.shape.w)
// side: above places this label above the horizontal connector, while
// near: start keeps it near the decision rather than near the repair shape.
label no(failure, "no") { near: start, side: above }

// ---- repair loop --------------------------------------------
// Proposed route property: route: curved requests a smooth connector.
// Its endpoints remain the repair's north port and the add shape's east
// port. Locus chooses the curve controls and boundary approach directions.
arrow retry(repair.shape.n, add.shape.e) { route: curved }

// avoid(path, obstacle, ...): keep the entire routed path clear of every
// listed obstacle. For shapes, avoid their interiors and outlines; for
// connectors, avoid crossings and overlapping strokes. Locus chooses
// clearance from the active style and adjusts this path's route, without
// moving the obstacles. The source and target ports remain attached.
// Report a routing conflict if no permitted curve satisfies the requirements.
rel avoid(retry, init.shape, check.shape, decision.shape, render.shape, failure)
